-- title:
--   p. 4 — the interval exchange transformations form a group
-- statement:
--   A permutation $g$ of $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$ lies in $\mathrm{IET}$, the subgroup generated by the interval exchange transformations, if and only if $g$ is itself an interval exchange transformation: right-continuous, with finitely many angles $g(x) - x$ and finitely many points of discontinuity.
--
--   Juschenko, Matte Bon, Monod and de la Salle, p. 4: “The set of all interval exchange transformations is a group acting on $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$, denoted by IET.”
--
--   The definitions item defines $\mathrm{IET}$ as the generated subgroup; this statement says that the generated subgroup adds nothing, which is the content of “is a group”. The requirement of finitely many points of discontinuity reads the precise definition on p. 4 together with its opening phrase, “cutting this circle into finitely many intervals”; the definitions item explains why.
-- source:
--   Juschenko, K., Matte Bon, N., Monod, N. and de la Salle, M., Extensive amenability and an application to interval exchanges, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 38 (2018) 195–219, https://doi.org/10.1017/etds.2016.32 (arXiv:1503.04977v1, whose page numbers are used), p. 4, the group IET

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_IntervalExchange

open IntervalExchange
open scoped ENNReal

namespace JMMS

theorem mem_IET_iff (g : Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle) : g ∈ IET ↔ IsIntervalExchange g := by
  sorry

end JMMS
